IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `vca-core`
IMPORT `vca-seat`

-- Cases for the applicant's seat (vca-seat.l4) and the regulative rules of the core (vca-core.l4): who can apply,
-- the heads, the exclusions and other schemes, s 5(4), what is paid, the time limits, reg 5, and the traces. The
-- prescribed maxima and their indexation are in vca-cases-maxima.l4. Every expected value was worked by hand from the
-- deposited Act (consolidation of 2023-12-08) and Regulations (2021-12-14), never copied from the encoding's output;
-- the reasoning or arithmetic is in the comment above each assertion. No scenario life covers Tasmania, so every
-- person here is HYPOTHETICAL, in Hobart.
--
-- A breached or still-pending DEONTIC cannot be asserted on the stable l4, so the regulative rules are asserted where
-- the trace ends FULFILLED, and #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line where it does not. Clock: days from the offence.
--
-- Argument order of "Can I apply, and as which kind of victim" (14 answers): an offence or excused; violence; a
-- listed Criminal Code crime; date of the offence; committed against me; injured assisting police; impairment of my
-- health; pregnant; injury from witnessing; my relationship; primary victim under 18; primary victim killed or
-- injured; primary victim died; I paid the funeral.

`2 Mar 2025`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2025
`1 Jun 2020`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2020
`1 Jun 2014`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2014
`1 Nov 2015`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 11 2015
`31 Oct 2015` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 31 10 2015

-- The kinds of applicant, abbreviated.
`PV`        MEANS `a primary victim (s 2)`
`SV witness` MEANS `a secondary victim injured by witnessing the offence (s 2, para (a))`
`SV parent` MEANS `a secondary victim as parent, step-parent or guardian of a primary victim under 18 (s 2, para (b))`
`RV`        MEANS `a related victim (s 2)`
`FP`        MEANS `a person who incurred a primary victim's funeral expenses (s 4(1A))`

-- The heads, abbreviated.
`own expenses`     MEANS `expenses actually and reasonably incurred as a result of my own injury`
`other expenses`   MEANS `expenses, other than funeral expenses, actually and reasonably incurred as a result of the death or injury of the primary victim`
`future with dental` MEANS `the cost of medical, dental, psychological or counselling services I will require in the future`
`future`           MEANS `the cost of medical, psychological or counselling services I will require in the future`
`own wages`        MEANS `loss of wages or salary from my total or partial incapacity for work arising from my own injury`
`caring wages`     MEANS `loss of wages or salary from my total or partial inability to work while providing care to the primary victim`
`pain`             MEANS `pain and suffering arising from my own injury`
`dependency`       MEANS `financial loss, if the primary victim died and I was dependent on the primary victim`
`claim costs`      MEANS `expenses reasonably incurred in claiming compensation`
`funeral`          MEANS `funeral expenses actually and reasonably incurred, or reasonably likely to be incurred, in relation to the death of the primary victim`

`none listed` MEANS `none of the relationships s 2 names`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 1. Who can apply, and as which kind (ss 2, 4(1)-(1B)). HYPOTHETICAL people; an assault in Hobart on 2 March
-- 2025 unless stated.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Alex is punched in a bar and his jaw broken: an offence involving violence, committed against him, with an
-- impairment of bodily health. A primary victim (s 2), injured (s 4(1)(a), (2)).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `PV`)
-- Bea saw it and developed post-traumatic stress: "a person who suffers injury as a result of witnessing an offence",
-- s 2, secondary victim (a).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `SV witness`)
-- Carol is the mother of Dan, 15, who is assaulted and injured; Carol is not injured herself. "a parent ... of a
-- primary victim who was under 18 years of age at the time of the offence": secondary victim (b), without any injury
-- of her own (NOTES.md finding 3).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `parent` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `SV parent`)
-- Ed is the father of a primary victim aged 25: "a parent or step-parent of a primary victim who was over 18 years of
-- age", related victim (b). The same answer for a primary victim of exactly 18 (READING: not "under 18"): the input
-- is the same.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `parent` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`)
-- A step-parent of an adult primary victim: related victim (b) too.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `step-parent` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`)
-- Fay, Alex's wife, saw the assault and was injured by it: secondary victim (a) and related victim (a), both (NOTES.md
-- finding 4). The list is in the order of s 2.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE `spouse` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `SV witness`, `RV`)
-- Gus's brother was murdered on 1 June 2020 and Gus paid for the funeral: a related victim (a), and a person who
-- incurred the funeral expenses of a primary victim who died as a result of criminal conduct on or after 1 November
-- 2015 (s 4(1A)-(1B)).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jun 2020` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `brother` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`, `FP`)
-- The same murder on 1 June 2014: before 1 November 2015, so no funeral expenses (s 4(1B)); still a related victim.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jun 2014` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `brother` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`)
-- s 4(1B) "on or after the day on which that subsection commenced": conduct on 1 November 2015 qualifies, on 31
-- October 2015 it does not.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Nov 2015` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `brother` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`, `FP`)
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `31 Oct 2015` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `brother` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`)
-- Hal, a friend, paid for the funeral of a person murdered in 2014: he is no kind of victim, and s 4(1B) shuts the
-- funeral door.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jun 2014` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no award: funeral expenses may be awarded only for criminal conduct on or after 1 November 2015 (s 4(1B))`
-- The same friend for a 2020 murder: "to a person for funeral expenses": he need not be a victim.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jun 2020` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `FP`)
-- Ivy lost her savings to a fraud and suffered anxiety: an offence, but without violence and not a listed crime, so
-- not an "offence" within s 2.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE FALSE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no award: there was no offence within s 2, nor an act that would have been one but for an excuse (s 4(1)(a))`
-- Nia was raped (a crime under s 185 of the Criminal Code, listed in s 2 whatever the violence answer) and became
-- pregnant, with no other impairment answered: "becoming pregnant" is an injury (s 2(2)). Primary victim.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE FALSE TRUE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `PV`)
-- Assaulted by a 9-year-old, who is below the age of criminal responsibility: "would have constituted an offence, but
-- for the fact that that other person had not attained a specified age" (s 4(1)(a)). Primary victim (READING: NOTES.md
-- finding 2; the first answer is yes).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `PV`)
-- Ian helped police catch a burglar and broke his arm in a fall; the burglar did nothing to him. s 4(1)(b) allows an
-- award, but no offence was committed against Ian, so he is not a primary victim, and ss 4(2)-(4) and reg 4 give him
-- no heads and no maximum (NOTES.md finding 1).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE FALSE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: injured in assisting a police officer, with no offence against me (s 4(1)(b)); ss 4(2)-(4) and reg 4 set no heads or maximum for me`
-- Jo helped police arrest a man who punched her: an offence against her, so a primary victim.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `PV`)
-- Kim is the guardian of an injured primary victim aged 30: secondary victim (b) needs a primary victim under 18, and
-- related victim (b) names only a parent or step-parent. Neither.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `guardian` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no award: I am not a primary, secondary or related victim, nor a person who incurred a primary victim's funeral expenses`
-- Lou is the grandparent of an injured child: s 2 does not name grandparents. Neither.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none listed` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no award: I am not a primary, secondary or related victim, nor a person who incurred a primary victim's funeral expenses`
-- A grandchild who was wholly dependent on a murdered grandparent: s 4(4)(f) is a related victim's head, and a
-- grandchild is not a related victim (NOTES.md finding 5).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jun 2020` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no award: I am not a primary, secondary or related victim, nor a person who incurred a primary victim's funeral expenses`
-- Max fought off an attempted assault without any impairment of his health: a primary victim (an offence was
-- committed against him), but there is no injury in respect of which s 4(2) allows an award.
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none listed` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no award: I am within s 2, but there is no death or injury in respect of which compensation may be awarded to me (s 4(1)-(4))`
-- The mother of a child who was threatened but not hurt, and was not hurt herself: a secondary victim (b), with no
-- death or injury to found an award (s 4(3)).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `parent` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no award: I am within s 2, but there is no death or injury in respect of which compensation may be awarded to me (s 4(1)-(4))`
-- A person in a personal relationship under the Relationships Act 2003 with an injured primary victim: related (c).
#ASSERT `Can I apply, and as which kind of victim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `2 Mar 2025` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `in a personal relationship within the meaning of the Relationships Act 2003` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `may apply as` (LIST `RV`)

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 2. The heads open to each kind (s 4(1A)-(4)). Arguments: kind; impairment of my health; pregnant; my
-- relationship; primary victim died; I was dependent.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Alex, primary victim: s 4(2)(a)-(e).
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `PV` TRUE FALSE `none listed` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `own expenses`, `future with dental`, `own wages`, `pain`, `claim costs`
-- Bea, secondary victim by witnessing, not a parent: s 4(3)(a), (c), (d)(i), (d)(ii), (e), (f); not (b). Future
-- services without "dental" (NOTES.md finding 6).
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `SV witness` TRUE FALSE `none listed` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `own expenses`, `future`, `own wages`, `caring wages`, `pain`, `claim costs`
-- Carol, secondary victim as Dan's mother, not injured herself: s 4(3)(b), (c), (d)(ii), (f). (a), (d)(i) and (e)
-- need her "own injury".
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `SV parent` FALSE FALSE `parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `other expenses`, `future`, `caring wages`, `claim costs`
-- The same, but Carol was injured too (she was assaulted trying to stop it): all of s 4(3).
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `SV parent` TRUE FALSE `parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `own expenses`, `other expenses`, `future`, `own wages`, `caring wages`, `pain`, `claim costs`
-- A secondary victim by witnessing who is the parent of the adult primary victim: s 4(3)(b) sets no age, so (b) too.
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `SV witness` TRUE FALSE `parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `own expenses`, `other expenses`, `future`, `own wages`, `caring wages`, `pain`, `claim costs`
-- Gus, related victim, brother murdered, not dependent, not injured: s 4(4)(b), (c), (d)(ii), (g).
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `RV` FALSE FALSE `brother` TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `other expenses`, `future`, `caring wages`, `claim costs`
-- The murdered man's wife, who depended on him: s 4(4)(f) as well.
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `RV` FALSE FALSE `spouse` TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `other expenses`, `future`, `caring wages`, `dependency`, `claim costs`
-- A dependent child of a primary victim who was injured but did not die: no s 4(4)(f) ("if the primary victim dies").
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `RV` FALSE FALSE `child` FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `other expenses`, `future`, `caring wages`, `claim costs`
-- Fay, related victim, injured herself, husband injured not killed: s 4(4)(a)-(e), (g).
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `RV` TRUE FALSE `spouse` FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `own expenses`, `other expenses`, `future`, `own wages`, `caring wages`, `pain`, `claim costs`
-- Gus, as the person who paid for the funeral: s 4(1A) only.
#ASSERT `Which heads of compensation can I claim` `FP` FALSE FALSE `brother` TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `funeral`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 3. Exclusions and the other schemes (ss 5(2), 5(3A), 6, 12). Arguments of "Is my claim excluded": heads I claim;
-- heads paid or payable under workers compensation; motor vehicle; property; exemplary damages; Medicare or health
-- fund; the Commissioner satisfied (s 5(2)); failed to help police (s 5(3A)); before the Act commenced (s 12).
-- =====================================================================================================================

`Alex's heads` MEANS LIST `own expenses`, `own wages`, `pain`

-- Alex claims his expenses, lost wages and pain and suffering; nothing excludes them.
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not excluded`
-- Olga was deliberately run down by a car: "a death or injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor vehicle"
-- (s 6(1)(a)). Wholly excluded; the MAIB scheme instead (NOTES.md finding 8).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 6(1)(a): the death or injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor vehicle`)
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` TRUE `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE EQUALS LIST `the MAIB scheme under the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and Compensation) Act 1973`
-- Her husband, who saw it and was injured (a secondary victim by witnessing): s 6(1)(a) speaks of "a death or injury",
-- so it reaches him too.
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` (LIST `own expenses`, `future`, `pain`) EMPTY TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 6(1)(a): the death or injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor vehicle`)
-- The person who paid for the funeral of someone killed by a car used as a weapon: excluded too ("Compensation must not
-- be awarded under this Act ... in respect of a death ... arising out of the use of a motor vehicle").
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` (LIST `funeral`) EMPTY TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 6(1)(a): the death or injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor vehicle`)
-- Alex was assaulted at work by a customer; workers compensation paid his lost wages. s 6(1)(d) excludes only "any
-- matter ... for which compensation has been paid, or is payable, under a compensation law": the wages go, his
-- expenses and pain and suffering stay (NOTES.md finding 9).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` (LIST `own wages`) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `partly excluded` (LIST `own expenses`, `pain`) (LIST `s 6(1)(d): matters paid, or payable, under a workers compensation law`)
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` FALSE `Alex's heads` (LIST `own wages`) FALSE EQUALS LIST `workers compensation under the compensation law that pays or must pay it`
-- Workers compensation pays or must pay all three: wholly excluded under s 6(1)(d).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` `Alex's heads` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 6(1)(d): matters paid, or payable, under a workers compensation law`)
-- Pat was killed at work in an assault; his employer's workers compensation paid a funeral benefit to his sister, who
-- paid for the funeral. s 6(1)(d) reaches only matters "referred to in section 4(2), (3) or (4)"; funeral expenses are
-- under s 4(1A). Not excluded, and no other scheme is pointed to (NOTES.md finding 7; s 5(4) still makes the
-- Commissioner take what she received into account).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` (LIST `funeral`) (LIST `funeral`) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not excluded`
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` FALSE (LIST `funeral`) (LIST `funeral`) FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
-- In the Commissioner's opinion Alex refused, without reason, to give police a statement: s 5(3A), no award.
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 5(3A): I failed to do what I reasonably should have done to help identify, apprehend or prosecute the offender`)
-- The Commissioner is not satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the injury resulted from criminal conduct
-- (s 5(2)).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 5(2): the Commissioner is not satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the death or injury was the result of criminal conduct`)
-- Alex also claims for his broken phone, for aggravated damages, and for GP visits Medicare covers: s 6(1)(b), (c)
-- and (e) take out those parts; every head stays.
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `partly excluded` `Alex's heads` (LIST `s 6(1)(b): exemplary, vindictive or aggravated damages`, `s 6(1)(c): loss of or damage to property`, `s 6(1)(e): expenses claimable under Medicare or from a registered health fund`)
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` FALSE `Alex's heads` EMPTY TRUE EQUALS LIST `Medicare or the registered health fund`
-- Abuse in 1970, before the Act commenced: s 7(1D) lifts the time limit for child sexual abuse, but s 12 still bars
-- the award (NOTES.md finding 12).
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 12: criminal conduct before the Act commenced`)
-- A motor vehicle and workers compensation together: wholly excluded by s 6(1)(a), with s 6(1)(d) also listed; both
-- other schemes, and Medicare.
#ASSERT `Is my claim excluded` `Alex's heads` (LIST `own wages`) TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `wholly excluded` (LIST `s 6(1)(a): the death or injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor vehicle`, `s 6(1)(d): matters paid, or payable, under a workers compensation law`)
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` TRUE `Alex's heads` (LIST `own wages`) TRUE EQUALS LIST `the MAIB scheme under the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and Compensation) Act 1973`, `workers compensation under the compensation law that pays or must pay it`, `Medicare or the registered health fund`
-- Nothing points elsewhere.
#ASSERT `Does my claim belong to MAIB or workers compensation instead` FALSE `Alex's heads` EMPTY FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 5(4): the Commissioner is satisfied Alex could sue the offender, and he has recovered $12 000 in damages: the
-- Commissioner may refuse, and must take the $12 000 into account in fixing any award.
#ASSERT `What does a civil claim do to my award` TRUE 12000 EQUALS (`Civil remedy answer` WITH `the Commissioner may refuse an award because I have, or had, an adequate civil remedy (s 5(4))` IS TRUE, `the damages or compensation the Commissioner must take into account in fixing the amount (s 5(4))` IS 12000)
-- No civil remedy and nothing recovered.
#ASSERT `What does a civil claim do to my award` FALSE 0 EQUALS (`Civil remedy answer` WITH `the Commissioner may refuse an award because I have, or had, an adequate civil remedy (s 5(4))` IS FALSE, `the damages or compensation the Commissioner must take into account in fixing the amount (s 5(4))` IS 0)

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 4. What is paid on an award (ss 6A(4), 6B). Arguments: the most I can be awarded; assessed under the other
-- heads; assessed for future services; future services on top; deductions.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Alex: maximum $33 736.50 (vca-cases-maxima.l4, FY 2025-26); the Commissioner assesses $40 000 under the other heads
-- and $8 000 for future counselling, determines the counselling is paid on top (s 6A(4)), and deducts a $1 500 fine
-- Alex owes (s 6B(1)(a)). Award = the smaller of 40 000 and 33 736.50, plus 8 000 = 41 736.50; paid 41 736.50 - 1 500
-- = 40 236.50. The fine is treated as paid to that extent (s 6B(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `What would be paid to me on an award` 33736.5 40000 8000 TRUE 1500 EQUALS (`Payment answer` WITH `the award` IS 41736.5, `deducted for penalties, levies and compensation orders I owe, which are treated as paid to that extent (s 6B)` IS 1500, `paid to me` IS 40236.5)
-- The same, but the counselling is not paid on top: the smaller of 48 000 and 33 736.50 = 33 736.50; paid 32 236.50.
#ASSERT `What would be paid to me on an award` 33736.5 40000 8000 FALSE 1500 EQUALS (`Payment answer` WITH `the award` IS 33736.5, `deducted for penalties, levies and compensation orders I owe, which are treated as paid to that extent (s 6B)` IS 1500, `paid to me` IS 32236.5)
-- Under the maximum: 12 000 + 3 000 = 15 000, nothing deducted.
#ASSERT `What would be paid to me on an award` 30000 12000 3000 FALSE 0 EQUALS (`Payment answer` WITH `the award` IS 15000, `deducted for penalties, levies and compensation orders I owe, which are treated as paid to that extent (s 6B)` IS 0, `paid to me` IS 15000)
-- An award of $2 000 against $3 000 owed under a compensation order: the deduction can take only the award; nothing
-- is paid, and the order is discharged to $2 000 (s 6B(2)(c)).
#ASSERT `What would be paid to me on an award` 5000 2000 0 FALSE 3000 EQUALS (`Payment answer` WITH `the award` IS 2000, `deducted for penalties, levies and compensation orders I owe, which are treated as paid to that extent (s 6B)` IS 2000, `paid to me` IS 0)
-- Gus's funeral claim: the funeral maximum left is $5 245.50 (vca-cases-maxima.l4); the funeral cost $9 000: paid
-- $5 245.50.
#ASSERT `What would be paid to me on an award` 5245.5 9000 0 FALSE 0 EQUALS (`Payment answer` WITH `the award` IS 5245.5, `deducted for penalties, levies and compensation orders I owe, which are treated as paid to that extent (s 6B)` IS 0, `paid to me` IS 5245.5)

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 5. Time limits (s 7(1A)-(1D), (8)-(9)). Arguments of "By when must I apply": date of the offence; my date of
-- birth; a primary, secondary or related victim; child sexual abuse; extended; the extended last day.
-- =====================================================================================================================

`10 Mar 2024` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 10 3 2024
`10 Mar 2027` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 10 3 2027
`11 Mar 2027` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 11 3 2027
`1 Jan 1990`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 1990
`5 May 2010`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 5 5 2010
`1 Jun 2024`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2024
`4 May 2028`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 4 5 2028
`5 May 2028`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 5 5 2028
`5 May 2031`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 5 5 2031
`1 Jan 1985`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 1985
`1 Jan 2008`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2008
`1 Jan 2022`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2022
`1 Jan 2025`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2025
`1 Jan 2029`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2029
`1 Jun 2027`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2027
`29 Feb 2024` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 29 2 2024
`28 Feb 2027` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 28 2 2027
`30 Jun 2028` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 30 6 2028
`1 May 2028`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 5 2028
`1 Jul 2028`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 7 2028
`1 Sep 2025`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 9 2025
`1 Mar 2023`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 3 2023
`1 Jul 2025`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 7 2025
`1 Aug 2025`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 8 2025
`1 Aug 2028`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 8 2028
`1 Feb 2029`  MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 2 2029

-- Alex, born 1990, assaulted 10 March 2024: "within 3 years after the date of the relevant offence" (s 7(1A)), to 10
-- March 2027.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `10 Mar 2027`
-- Dan, born 5 May 2010, assaulted at 14 on 1 June 2024: "no later than 3 years after he or she turns 18" (s 7(1B)): he
-- turns 18 on 5 May 2028, so 5 May 2031.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `1 Jun 2024` `5 May 2010` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `no later than 3 years after I turn 18 (s 7(1B))` `5 May 2031`
-- Assaulted on 4 May 2028, the day before his 18th birthday: still "less than 18", so 5 May 2031 (under s 7(1A) it
-- would be 4 May 2031).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `4 May 2028` `5 May 2010` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `no later than 3 years after I turn 18 (s 7(1B))` `5 May 2031`
-- Assaulted on his 18th birthday: not less than 18, so s 7(1A): 5 May 2031.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `5 May 2028` `5 May 2010` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `5 May 2031`
-- Carol, Dan's mother (born 1985), a secondary victim: s 7(1B) turns on her own age, so she has 3 years from the
-- offence, to 1 June 2027, though Dan has until 2031 (NOTES.md finding 11).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `1 Jun 2024` `1 Jan 1985` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `1 Jun 2027`
-- An application that involves an allegation of child sexual abuse: s 7(1A) and (1B) do not apply (s 7(1D)).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `1 Jan 1985` `1 Jan 1985` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `no time limit: the application involves an allegation of child sexual abuse (s 7(1D))`
-- An offence on 29 February 2024: READING, 28 February 2027 (NOTES.md finding 20).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `29 Feb 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `28 Feb 2027`
-- The Commissioner extends Alex's time to 30 June 2028 for special circumstances (s 7(1C)).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE TRUE `30 Jun 2028` EQUALS `as extended by the Commissioner for special circumstances (s 7(1C))` `30 Jun 2028`
-- Quinn's uncle was stabbed on 1 January 2022 and died of the wounds on 1 June 2025; Quinn, a nephew and so not a
-- related victim, paid for the funeral. Time runs from "the date of the relevant offence": the period ended on 1
-- January 2025, before the death (NOTES.md finding 10).
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `1 Jan 2022` `1 Jan 1990` FALSE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `1 Jan 2025`
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `1 Jan 2022` `1 Jan 1990` FALSE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` `1 Jul 2025` EQUALS `out of time: no award unless the Commissioner extends the period for special circumstances (s 7(1C))`
-- The uncle's son Rob, born 1 January 2008 and so 13 at the stabbing, is a related victim: he has until 3 years after
-- turning 18 (1 January 2026), so 1 January 2029, for his own heads and, as the same related victim, for the funeral.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply` `1 Jan 2022` `1 Jan 2008` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` EQUALS `no later than 3 years after I turn 18 (s 7(1B))` `1 Jan 2029`

-- In time: Alex lodges on 10 March 2027, the last day: in time; on 11 March 2027: out of time.
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` `10 Mar 2027` EQUALS `in time (s 7(1A)-(1D))`
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` `11 Mar 2027` EQUALS `out of time: no award unless the Commissioner extends the period for special circumstances (s 7(1C))`
-- Extended to 30 June 2028: lodged 1 May 2028, in time; lodged 1 July 2028, out of time.
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE TRUE `30 Jun 2028` `1 May 2028` EQUALS `in time, within the period as the Commissioner extended it (s 7(1C))`
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE FALSE TRUE `30 Jun 2028` `1 Jul 2028` EQUALS `out of time: no award unless the Commissioner extends the period for special circumstances (s 7(1C))`
-- Child sexual abuse in 1985, lodged 1 September 2025: in time (s 7(1D); s 7(11)(b) applies the amended section even
-- where the old period had expired).
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `1 Jan 1985` `1 Jan 1985` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` `1 Sep 2025` EQUALS `in time (s 7(1A)-(1D))`
-- Lodged on 1 March 2023, before s 7 took its present form on 20 April 2023: s 5(9) applies the Act as then in force,
-- which is not deposited.
#ASSERT `Is my application in time` `10 Mar 2024` `1 Jan 1990` TRUE TRUE FALSE `1 Jan 1990` `1 Mar 2023` EQUALS `not covered: lodged before 20 April 2023, when s 7 was in an earlier form (s 5(9)) that is not deposited`

-- s 7(8): lodged 1 August 2025, "to be determined within 3 years of the date of the application": 1 August 2028.
#ASSERT `By when is my application to be decided` `1 Aug 2025` FALSE `1 Aug 2025` EQUALS `1 Aug 2028`
-- Extended by the Commissioner to 1 February 2029 (s 7(9)).
#ASSERT `By when is my application to be decided` `1 Aug 2025` TRUE `1 Feb 2029` EQUALS `1 Feb 2029`
-- Lodged 29 February 2024: READING, 28 February 2027.
#ASSERT `By when is my application to be decided` `29 Feb 2024` FALSE `29 Feb 2024` EQUALS `28 Feb 2027`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 6. What the application must contain (reg 5). Arguments: kind; on behalf of another; because of a death;
-- injured assisting police.
-- =====================================================================================================================

`5(1)` MEANS `reg 5(1): lodge it with the Victim Assistance Unit, with the particulars in reg 5(1)(a)-(k)`
`5(2)` MEANS `reg 5(2): details of the funeral expenses actually and reasonably incurred, or reasonably likely to be incurred`
`5(3)` MEANS `reg 5(3): the claimant's details, your relationship, why you apply for them and, if asked, their consent`
`5(4)` MEANS `reg 5(4): the primary victim's details, your relationship, and what is known of their claim for damages and injuries`
`5(5)` MEANS `reg 5(5): a statement of the circumstances in which you witnessed the offence`
`5(6)` MEANS `reg 5(6): the deceased's details, your relationship, the expenses from the death and, if a dependant, your other financial loss`
`5(7)` MEANS `reg 5(7): the police officer's name and rank, the circumstances and the assistance given`
`5(8)` MEANS `reg 5(8)-(9): a statutory declaration in the form in Schedule 1`

-- Alex, a primary victim applying for himself: reg 5(1) and the statutory declaration (5(8)-(9)).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `PV` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `5(1)`, `5(8)`
-- Gus, for the funeral after a death: 5(1), 5(2), 5(6), 5(8).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `FP` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `5(1)`, `5(2)`, `5(6)`, `5(8)`
-- Bea, a witness: 5(1), 5(4) (secondary victim), 5(5), 5(8).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `SV witness` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `5(1)`, `5(4)`, `5(5)`, `5(8)`
-- Jo's father applies for her, a primary victim injured assisting police: 5(1), 5(3), 5(7), 5(8).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `PV` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `5(1)`, `5(3)`, `5(7)`, `5(8)`
-- The murdered man's wife, a related victim: 5(1), 5(4), 5(6), 5(8).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `RV` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `5(1)`, `5(4)`, `5(6)`, `5(8)`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CASE 7. Regulative rules (core): s 7(1A)-(1D), (8)-(9), with ss 5(2), 5(3A), 6 and 6A as the limit on the award.
-- Clock: days from the offence. Alex's offence: 10 March 2024 = day 0.
--   Last day to apply, 10 March 2027: 3 x 365 = day 1095 (no 29 February falls between).
--   Lodged 1 August 2025 = day 509 (365 to 10 March 2025; then 21 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 1 = 144).
--   To be determined by 1 August 2028: 1096 days later (29 February 2028 falls between), day 509 + 1096 = 1605.
--   Lodged 10 March 2027 (day 1095): to be determined by 10 March 2030, 1096 days later, day 2191.
-- =====================================================================================================================

`Alex's period` MEANS `within 3 years after the date of the offence (s 7(1A))` `10 Mar 2027`
`three years`   MEANS `within 3 years of the date of the application (s 7(8))`
`10 Mar 2027 lodging` MEANS `lodge an application for an award` `10 Mar 2027`
`11 Mar 2027 lodging` MEANS `lodge an application for an award` `11 Mar 2027`
`1 Aug 2025 lodging`  MEANS `lodge an application for an award` `1 Aug 2025`

-- Lodged day 509; determined day 900 with $30 000, within the $33 736.50 maximum; the prohibition's period passes at
-- day 1605: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Aug 2025 lodging` 509), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 30000) 900), (WAITUNTIL 1700)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- Lodged on the last day (day 1095); determined day 2000; period passes at day 2191: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `10 Mar 2027 lodging` 1095), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 20000) 2000), (WAITUNTIL 2200)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- Determined on day 1605, the last day: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Aug 2025 lodging` 509), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 33736.5) 1605), (WAITUNTIL 1700)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- A refusal (an award of 0) is a determination: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Aug 2025 lodging` 509), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 0) 700), (WAITUNTIL 1700)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the applicant, "s 7(1A)-(1C): the application was not made within the period ..." (lodged day 1096)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `11 Mar 2027 lodging` AT 1096
-- expect: BREACH BY the Commissioner, "ss 5(2), 5(3A), 5(10), 6, 6A: the award is more than the Act allows ..." ($40 000)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `1 Aug 2025 lodging` AT 509
    PARTY `the Commissioner` DOES `determine the application, awarding` 40000 AT 900
-- expect: BREACH BY the Commissioner, "s 7(8): the application was not determined within 3 years ..." (nothing by day 1606)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `1 Aug 2025 lodging` AT 509
    (WAITUNTIL 1606)

-- s 7(9): the Commissioner extends the time to determine to 1 February 2029, 1280 days after 1 August 2025 (1096 to 1
-- August 2028, then 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 = 184): day 509 + 1280 = 1789. Determined day 1700: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` (`as extended by the Commissioner for special circumstances (s 7(9))` `1 Feb 2029`) 33736.5) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Aug 2025 lodging` 509), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 25000) 1700), (WAITUNTIL 1800)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the Commissioner, "s 7(8): ..." (without the extension, day 1700 is after day 1605)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` 33736.5 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `1 Aug 2025 lodging` AT 509
    PARTY `the Commissioner` DOES `determine the application, awarding` 25000 AT 1700

-- Olga, run down by a car (s 6(1)(a)): the most the Commissioner may award is nothing. A refusal is fulfilled; any
-- award is a breach.
GIVEN `a motor vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Exclusion facts`
`exclusion facts with a motor vehicle answer of` `a motor vehicle` MEANS `Exclusion facts` WITH
    `the Commissioner is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the death or injury was the result of criminal conduct` IS TRUE
    `in the Commissioner's opinion the applicant failed to do what they reasonably should have to assist in the identification, apprehension or prosecution of the offender` IS FALSE
    `the death or injury was caused by or arose out of the use of a motor vehicle`  IS `a motor vehicle`
    `exemplary, vindictive or aggravated damages are claimed`                        IS FALSE
    `loss of or damage to property is claimed`                                       IS FALSE
    `the heads claimed`                                                              IS `Alex's heads`
    `the heads paid, or payable, under a compensation law`                           IS EMPTY
    `expenses claimable under Medicare or from a registered health benefits organisation are claimed` IS FALSE
    `the criminal conduct occurred before the Act commenced`                         IS FALSE
`Olga's exclusion facts` MEANS `exclusion facts with a motor vehicle answer of` TRUE
#ASSERT `the most the Commissioner may award` `Olga's exclusion facts` 33736.5 0 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` (`the most the Commissioner may award` `Olga's exclusion facts` 33736.5 0)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Aug 2025 lodging` 509), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 0) 600), (WAITUNTIL 1700)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the Commissioner, "ss 5(2), 5(3A), 5(10), 6, 6A: ..." (an award of $5 000 where s 6(1)(a) excludes)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `10 Mar 2024` `Alex's period` `three years` (`the most the Commissioner may award` `Olga's exclusion facts` 33736.5 0) AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `1 Aug 2025 lodging` AT 509
    PARTY `the Commissioner` DOES `determine the application, awarding` 5000 AT 600
-- With future counselling of $8 000 paid on top (s 6A(4)), and no exclusion, the Commissioner may award 33 736.50 + 8
-- 000 = 41 736.50.
#ASSERT `the most the Commissioner may award` (`exclusion facts with a motor vehicle answer of` FALSE) 33736.5 8000 EQUALS 41736.5

-- Child sexual abuse in 1985 (s 7(1D)): no period to apply. Offence 1 January 1985 = day 0; 1 September 2025 is day
-- 14853 (40 x 365 + 10 leap days = 14610 to 1 January 2025, plus 243). To be determined by 1 September 2028, 1096 days
-- later, day 15949. Determined day 15000: fulfilled.
`1 Sep 2025 lodging` MEANS `lodge an application for an award` `1 Sep 2025`
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `1 Jan 1985` `no time limit: the application involves an allegation of child sexual abuse (s 7(1D))` `three years` 30000) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `1 Sep 2025 lodging` 14853), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 30000) 15000), (WAITUNTIL 15950)) EQUALS FULFILLED

-- Dan (s 7(1B)): offence 1 June 2024 = day 0; last day 5 May 2031 = day 2529 (1 June 2031 is 7 x 365 + 1 = 2556 days on,
-- with 29 February 2028; 5 May is 27 days before 1 June). Lodged on 5 May 2031; to be determined by 5 May 2034, 1096
-- days later (29 February 2032 falls between), day 3625. Determined day 2600: fulfilled.
`Dan's period` MEANS `no later than 3 years after I turn 18 (s 7(1B))` `5 May 2031`
`5 May 2031 lodging` MEANS `lodge an application for an award` `5 May 2031`
`6 May 2031 lodging` MEANS `lodge an application for an award` (DATE_FROM_DMY 6 5 2031)
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `1 Jun 2024` `Dan's period` `three years` 22491) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `5 May 2031 lodging` 2529), (EVENT `the Commissioner` (`determine the application, awarding` 15000) 2600), (WAITUNTIL 3626)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the applicant, "s 7(1A)-(1C): ..." (lodged day 2530, 6 May 2031)
#TRACE `s 7 -- apply within the period; the Commissioner then determines` `1 Jun 2024` `Dan's period` `three years` 22491 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the applicant` DOES `6 May 2031 lodging` AT 2530
